{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0174PABNoBhum71YK7sbyb7R","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\n151\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_rsa} summarizes the RSA alignment of each model component with action structure on LIBERO-Long.\n152\tThe SigLIP encoder plus projector achieves the highest RSA ($\\rho{=}0.615$), substantially exceeding the best LLM layer in all four VLA conditions ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and the DINOv2 baseline ($\\rho{=}0.388$).\n153\tThe $2{\\times}2$ matrix of VLA conditions converges to a narrow band: training changes RSA by at most $\\Delta{=}0.01$, and instruction presence changes RSA by at most $\\Delta{=}0.005$.\n154\tBootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for all pairwise differences include zero, confirming that neither factor has a statistically significant effect.\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[t]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\caption{RSA alignment ($\\rho$) with action structure on LIBERO-Long. The $2{\\times}2$ VLA conditions span a range of only 0.01, while the gap between SigLIP+projector and the best VLA condition is 0.127.}\n159\t\\label{tab:main_rsa}\n160\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n161\t\\toprule\n162\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ SigLIP+Proj} \\\\\n163\t\\midrule\n164\tSigLIP + Projector & 0.615 & --- \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\tTrained VLA + Instruction & 0.488 & $-$0.127 \\\\\n167\tTrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.484 & $-$0.131 \\\\\n168\tUntrained VLA + Instruction & 0.482 & $-$0.133 \\\\\n169\tUntrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.479 & $-$0.136 \\\\\n170\t\\midrule\n171\tDINOv2 & 0.388 & $-$0.227 \\\\\n172\t\\bottomrule\n173\t\\end{tabular}\n174\t\\end{table}\n175\t\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Per-Layer RSA Degradation Curve}\n178\t\\label{sec:exp_perlayer}\n179\t\n180\tTo provide mechanistic evidence for Claim~2, we compute RSA at each of the 33 LLM layers plus the projector output.\n181\tFigure~\\ref{fig:perlayer} (described below) and Table~\\ref{tab:perlayer} show that RSA decreases monotonically from the projector output ($\\rho{=}0.615$) through layer~0 ($\\rho{=}0.601$) to layer~32 ($\\rho{=}0.449$), with a total drop of 0.166.\n182\tThe monotonicity is statistically strong (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{=}1.2{\\times}10^{-11}$).\n183\t\n184\tThe degradation is not uniform: the steepest drop occurs in layers~0--12 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.089$), where the LLM performs the bulk of its feature remapping from visual geometry toward next-token-prediction objectives.\n185\tLayers~20--32 exhibit a plateau ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.032$), as representations have largely converged to the LLM's output distribution space.\n186\t\n187\tCritically, an \\emph{untrained} VLA (random LLM weights) exhibits a nearly identical degradation trajectory (layer~0: $\\rho{=}0.593$; layer~32: $\\rho{=}0.443$; monotonicity $\\rho{=}{-}0.82$).\n188\tThis demonstrates that degradation is an intrinsic property of the transformer architecture's representational transformations, not a product of specific learned weights.\n189\t\n190\t\\begin{table}[t]\n191\t\\centering\n192\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA ($\\rho$) for the trained VLA on LIBERO-Long. Degradation is steepest in layers 0--12 and plateaus in deeper layers.}\n193\t\\label{tab:perlayer}\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t\\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ from Proj.} & \\textbf{Region} \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tProjector Output & 0.615 & --- & --- \\\\\n199\tLayer 0  & 0.601 & $-$0.014 & \\multirow{4}{*}{Steep drop} \\\\\n200\tLayer 4  & 0.571 & $-$0.044 & \\\\\n201\tLayer 8  & 0.539 & $-$0.076 & \\\\\n202\tLayer 12 & 0.512 & $-$0.103 & \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tLayer 16 & 0.496 & $-$0.119 & \\multirow{2}{*}{Transition} \\\\\n205\tLayer 20 & 0.481 & $-$0.134 & \\\\\n206\t\\midrule\n207\tLayer 24 & 0.470 & $-$0.145 & \\multirow{3}{*}{Plateau} \\\\\n208\tLayer 28 & 0.458 & $-$0.157 & \\\\\n209\tLayer 32 & 0.449 & $-$0.166 & \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Monotonicity: Spearman $\\rho = -0.88$, $p = 1.2 \\times 10^{-11}$} \\\\\n212\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Untrained VLA: L0$=$0.593, L32$=$0.443, Spearman $\\rho = -0.82$} \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\t\n218\t\\subsection{Random Instruction Control}\n219\t\\label{sec:exp_random_inst}\n220\t\n221\tA remaining alternative explanation is that instruction \\emph{content} contributes to action-relevant structure---perhaps meaningful task descriptions provide semantic grounding that shapes the LLM's geometry.\n222\tWe test this by replacing real instructions with three types of meaningless input.\n223\t\n224\tTable~\\ref{tab:random_inst} shows that all five conditions (real instruction, random English, shuffled tokens, padding only, and no instruction) converge within a band of $[0.481,\\, 0.489]$ with $\\sigma{=}0.003$.\n225\tThe maximum difference between any random condition and real instruction is $\\Delta{=}0.004$, and the bootstrap 95\\% CI of this difference ($[-0.031,\\, 0.038]$) includes zero.\n226\tPer-layer RSA curves across conditions correlate at $r{=}0.97$.\n227\t\n228\tThis definitively rules out instruction content as a contributor to action-relevant representational structure.\n229\tThe LLM's geometry is determined by the dominant visual token pathway; instruction tokens---regardless of their semantic content, grammaticality, or even identity---constitute a negligible perturbation.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{RSA ($\\rho$) under five instruction conditions on LIBERO-Long. All conditions converge within a 0.008-wide band; no pairwise difference is statistically significant.}\n234\t\\label{tab:random_inst}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Peak RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Peak Layer} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ Real Inst.} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tReal Instruction    & 0.488 & --- & --- \\\\\n240\tNo Instruction      & 0.489 & --- & $+$0.001 \\\\\n241\tRandom English      & 0.484 & 11  & $-$0.004 \\\\\n242\tShuffled Tokens     & 0.486 & 10  & $-$0.002 \\\\\n243\tPadding Only        & 0.481 & 12  & $-$0.007 \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Range: $[0.481,\\, 0.489]$; $\\sigma = 0.003$; Bootstrap 95\\% CI of max $\\Delta$: $[-0.031,\\, 0.038]$} \\\\\n246\t\\bottomrule\n247\t\\end{tabular}\n248\t\\end{table}\n249\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFrS9JYr5QQdkwMT9ueEb4","session_id":"b41b243d-0e5c-47b1-83bb-7de38ac1f796","uuid":"c64cc91a-680a-41f6-8696-c569de7c156f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T23:07:22.397Z"}
