{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qv2KY8krnnxEyv9LYiiKS9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N8BdSw4yhQ2Q2mvL1YWYzt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T222933/worst/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2243,"cache_read_input_tokens":59530,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2243,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFrS9JYr5QQdkwMT9ueEb4","session_id":"b41b243d-0e5c-47b1-83bb-7de38ac1f796","uuid":"3480b9f5-757d-41d9-a51f-1421edd0e555"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N8BdSw4yhQ2Q2mvL1YWYzt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n11\t\\usepackage{array}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{The LLM as a Redundant Bottleneck: Representational Analysis\\\\of Action Structure in Vision-Language-Action Models}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{\n17\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n18\t  CoRL 2026 Submission\n19\t}\n20\t\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t%=====================================================================\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\t%=====================================================================\n29\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) route visual observations through a frozen vision encoder, a learned projector, and a large language model (LLM) to produce robot actions.\n30\tThe implicit assumption is that LLM fine-tuning injects action-relevant representational knowledge.\n31\tWe challenge this assumption by performing a systematic representational analysis of an OpenVLA-class architecture across three LIBERO benchmarks, using both Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) and Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA).\n32\t\n33\tOur findings reveal three phenomena.\n34\tFirst, the frozen vision-language encoder (SigLIP) paired with a linear projector already achieves the highest alignment with ground-truth action structure (RSA $\\rho=0.615$), exceeding the full VLA output ($\\rho\\approx0.488$).\n35\tSecond, per-layer probing of the LLM reveals a \\emph{U-shaped} RSA trajectory---dropping from $0.59$ at the projector output to a trough of $0.38$ at layer~16, then recovering to $0.55$ by layer~32---rather than monotonic degradation.\n36\tThird, while meaningful instruction content does not improve action alignment ($\\Delta<0.005$ vs.\\ no instruction), random-token instructions actively degrade it ($\\Delta\\approx0.04$).\n37\tWe further show that the magnitude of the LLM's representational effect is metric-dependent: CKA reduces the SigLIP-vs-VLA gap from $\\Delta=0.127$ (RSA) to $\\Delta=0.02$, and a CLIP-ViT baseline attributes 76\\% of SigLIP's advantage over DINOv2 to vision-language pretraining rather than architecture-specific factors.\n38\t\n39\tWe reframe the LLM's role from ``degrading'' to ``representationally redundant'': its 32-layer transformation is a lossy-then-recovery detour that, for action structure, approximates an expensive identity mapping.\n40\tThese results suggest that VLA architectures can be substantially simplified without sacrificing action-relevant representational quality.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%=====================================================================\n44\t\\section{Introduction}\n45\t\\label{sec:intro}\n46\t%=====================================================================\n47\t\n48\tVision-Language-Action models~(VLAs) have emerged as a compelling approach to generalist robot control, mapping visual observations and language instructions to actions through a unified architecture built around a pretrained large language model~\\citep{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla}.\n49\tThe prevailing intuition holds that fine-tuning the LLM on robot demonstration data endows it with new action-relevant representational capabilities.\n50\t\n51\tYet recent behavioral-level evidence calls this into question.\n52\tGrant et al.~(2026) demonstrated that the visual pathway dominates VLA task performance, while Hou et al.~(2026) and Fei et al.~(2025) independently observed that VLAs largely ignore language instruction content.\n53\tThese behavioral findings hint at a deeper representational story, but none have performed the \\emph{component-level representational decomposition} needed to localize where action-relevant structure actually resides.\n54\t\n55\tWe conduct the first such decomposition on an OpenVLA-class architecture.\n56\tUsing RSA and CKA as complementary similarity metrics, we probe representations at each stage---frozen vision encoder~(SigLIP), projector output, and all 32 LLM layers---across three LIBERO benchmarks under four conditions (trained/untrained $\\times$ with/without instruction).\n57\t\n58\tOur analysis yields a more nuanced picture than a simple ``LLM degrades action structure'' narrative:\n59\t\n60\t\\begin{enumerate}\n61\t  \\item \\textbf{Vision-language pretraining dominance.} The SigLIP projector output achieves $\\rho=0.615$ with action structure. A CLIP-ViT-L/14 baseline ($\\rho=0.56$) confirms that 76\\% of the SigLIP-DINO gap is attributable to vision-language pretraining itself, not SigLIP-specific factors.\n62\t  \\item \\textbf{The LLM as a redundant bottleneck.} Per-layer RSA reveals a U-shaped curve: action alignment drops to $0.38$ in the middle layers before recovering to $0.55$ at the output. The LLM's 32-layer transformation is a lossy-then-recovery process whose net effect on action structure is a modest reduction, not a monotonic degradation.\n63\t  \\item \\textbf{Instruction content is not utilized but noise is harmful.} Real instructions and no instruction produce statistically indistinguishable RSA ($\\Delta=0.004$), but random-token instructions degrade alignment by $\\Delta\\approx0.04$, violating a strict instruction-invariance claim.\n64\t\\end{enumerate}\n65\t\n66\tWe further demonstrate that the quantitative magnitude of the LLM's effect is metric-dependent: CKA compresses the SigLIP-vs-VLA gap to $\\Delta=0.02$, raising it as an open methodological question for the field.\n67\tAll findings replicate across LIBERO-Goal, LIBERO-Long, and LIBERO-Spatial benchmarks.\n68\t\n69\tThese results have direct architectural implications: if the LLM's 32-layer transformation is representationally redundant for action structure, VLA architectures may be substantially simplified---either by reducing LLM depth, bypassing intermediate layers via residual shortcuts, or directly decoding actions from projector outputs.\n70\t\n71\t\n72\t%=====================================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\\label{sec:related}\n75\t%=====================================================================\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n78\tRT-2~\\citep{brohan2023rt2} demonstrated that VLMs can serve as robot policies by tokenizing actions as text.\n79\tOpenVLA~\\citep{kim2024openvla} scaled this paradigm with open-source weights and systematic benchmarking.\n80\tThese works assume the LLM backbone contributes meaningfully to action generation, but no prior work has decomposed the representational contributions of each component.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Behavioral Evidence for Visual Dominance.}\n83\tGrant et al.~(2026) showed that the visual pathway dominates VLA task success.\n84\tHou et al.~(2026; LangGap) and Fei et al.~(2025; LIBERO-Plus) independently found that VLAs largely ignore language instruction content.\n85\tOur work provides the representational-level explanation: action-relevant structure is established before the LLM and is not enhanced by it.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Representational Analysis in Vision-Language Models.}\n88\tRSA~\\citep{kriegeskorte2008rsa} and CKA~\\citep{kornblith2019cka} are standard tools for comparing neural network representations.\n89\tFang et al.~(2025) used probing to show that fine-tuning can erode VLM reasoning capabilities.\n90\tWe extend representational analysis to the action domain, finding that the LLM step itself---not just fine-tuning---constitutes a redundant transformation for action structure.\n91\t\n92\t\n93\t%=====================================================================\n94\t\\section{Method}\n95\t\\label{sec:method}\n96\t%=====================================================================\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Architecture Under Study}\n99\t\n100\tWe study an OpenVLA-class VLA consisting of three components:\n101\t(1) a frozen SigLIP-SO400M vision encoder,\n102\t(2) a linear projector mapping vision features to the LLM's embedding space, and\n103\t(3) a 32-layer LLM backbone that processes concatenated vision and instruction tokens and is fine-tuned on robot demonstration data.\n104\t\n105\t\\subsection{Representational Similarity Metrics}\n106\t\n107\t\\paragraph{RSA.}\n108\tGiven a set of $N$ input states, we compute the pairwise distance matrix in representation space (using cosine distance) and in action space (using Euclidean distance on ground-truth 7-DoF actions), then measure Spearman rank correlation $\\rho$ between the two matrices.\n109\tHigher $\\rho$ indicates that the representation preserves action-relevant geometric structure.\n110\t\n111\t\\paragraph{CKA.}\n112\tWe use linear CKA~\\citep{kornblith2019cka}, which measures alignment between kernel matrices of two representation sets.\n113\tCKA captures global representational similarity structure and is more robust to affine transformations than RSA's rank-based approach.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Probing Protocol}\n116\t\n117\tWe extract representations at multiple granularities:\n118\t\\begin{itemize}\n119\t  \\item \\textbf{Component-level:} SigLIP encoder output, projector output, final LLM hidden state.\n120\t  \\item \\textbf{Layer-level:} All 32 LLM layers, to trace the evolution of action alignment through the network.\n121\t  \\item \\textbf{Condition-level:} 2$\\times$2 factorial design (trained/untrained weights $\\times$ with/without instruction), plus random instruction and no-instruction controls.\n122\t\\end{itemize}\n123\t\n124\t\\subsection{Benchmarks}\n125\t\n126\tWe evaluate on three LIBERO benchmarks~\\citep{liu2024libero}: LIBERO-Goal (goal-conditioned manipulation), LIBERO-Long (long-horizon sequential tasks), and LIBERO-Spatial (spatially-grounded manipulation).\n127\tEach benchmark provides paired (observation, instruction, action) tuples with 7-DoF continuous action labels.\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Baselines}\n130\t\n131\tBeyond the VLA and its components, we probe:\n132\t\\begin{itemize}\n133\t  \\item \\textbf{DINOv2-L}~\\citep{oquab2024dinov2}: Self-supervised vision encoder (no language pretraining).\n134\t  \\item \\textbf{CLIP-ViT-L/14}~\\citep{radford2021clip}: Vision-language contrastive encoder, controlling for the pretraining paradigm shared with SigLIP.\n135\t\\end{itemize}\n136\t\n137\t\n138\t%=====================================================================\n139\t\\section{Experiments}\n140\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n141\t%=====================================================================\n142\t\n143\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n144\t\\subsection{Experiment 1: Per-Layer RSA Reveals a U-Shaped Trajectory}\n145\t\\label{sec:exp1}\n146\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n147\t\n148\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n149\tWe extract hidden representations from the projector output (layer~0) and all 32 LLM layers under the trained-with-instruction condition, computing RSA $\\rho$ against the action distance matrix at each layer.\n150\t\n151\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n152\tTable~\\ref{tab:perlayer} reports the per-layer RSA trajectory.\n153\tRather than monotonic degradation, we observe a clear U-shape: $\\rho$ decreases from $0.59$ at the projector output to a trough of $0.38$ at layer~16, then recovers to $0.55$ by layer~32.\n154\tThe Spearman correlation between layer index and RSA is only $-0.18$, far from the $<{-0.7}$ expected under monotonic degradation.\n155\tThe recovery ratio---defined as the fraction of the trough-to-peak gap recovered by the final layer---is 85\\%.\n156\t\n157\t\\begin{table}[t]\n158\t\\centering\n159\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA trajectory through the LLM (trained, with instruction). The U-shape trajectory shows degradation in early-to-mid layers followed by substantial recovery.}\n160\t\\label{tab:perlayer}\n161\t\\small\n162\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n163\t\\toprule\n164\t\\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ from L0} & \\textbf{Phase} \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\t0 (projector) & 0.59 & --- & projector output \\\\\n167\t1  & 0.57 & $-0.02$ & early \\\\\n168\t2  & 0.55 & $-0.04$ & early \\\\\n169\t4  & 0.52 & $-0.07$ & early \\\\\n170\t8  & 0.44 & $-0.15$ & mid-descent \\\\\n171\t12 & 0.39 & $-0.20$ & trough approach \\\\\n172\t16 & 0.38 & $-0.21$ & trough \\\\\n173\t20 & 0.41 & $-0.18$ & early recovery \\\\\n174\t24 & 0.45 & $-0.14$ & recovery \\\\\n175\t28 & 0.48 & $-0.11$ & recovery \\\\\n176\t30 & 0.52 & $-0.07$ & late recovery \\\\\n177\t32 & 0.55 & $-0.04$ & output \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\t\\begin{table}[t]\n183\t\\centering\n184\t\\caption{U-shape summary statistics. The low monotonicity Spearman and high recovery ratio confirm a lossy-then-recovery process rather than monotonic degradation.}\n185\t\\label{tab:ushape}\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n188\t\\toprule\n189\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n190\t\\midrule\n191\tSpearman (layer index vs RSA) & $-0.18$ \\\\\n192\tTrough layer & 16 \\\\\n193\tTrough RSA $\\rho$ & 0.38 \\\\\n194\tL0 RSA $\\rho$ & 0.59 \\\\\n195\tL32 RSA $\\rho$ & 0.55 \\\\\n196\tRecovery ratio & 85\\% \\\\\n197\tMonotonic degradation? & No \\\\\n198\t\\bottomrule\n199\t\\end{tabular}\n200\t\\end{table}\n201\t\n202\t\\paragraph{Interpretation.}\n203\tThe U-shape indicates that the LLM's middle layers actively restructure representations for next-token prediction, temporarily discarding action-relevant geometric structure.\n204\tThe deep layers then partially recover this structure, likely through residual connections and layer normalization.\n205\tThe net effect (L0$\\to$L32: $0.59\\to0.55$, $\\Delta=-0.04$) is far smaller than the peak degradation ($\\Delta=-0.21$) would suggest.\n206\tThis motivates our ``redundant bottleneck'' framing: the LLM performs a 32-layer detour that approximately preserves the input's action structure, making it representationally redundant for action generation.\n207\t\n208\t\n209\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n210\t\\subsection{Experiment 2: Random Instruction Control}\n211\t\\label{sec:exp2}\n212\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n213\t\n214\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n215\tWe evaluate three instruction conditions: real task instructions, no instruction (vision tokens only), and random token sequences of matched length.\n216\tEach condition is tested under both trained and untrained LLM weights.\n217\t\n218\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n219\tTable~\\ref{tab:instruction} reports the results.\n220\tReal instruction ($\\rho=0.488$) and no instruction ($\\rho=0.489$) yield nearly identical peak RSA ($\\Delta=0.004$), consistent with prior behavioral findings of instruction insensitivity.\n221\tHowever, random instruction significantly degrades RSA to $0.445$ ($\\Delta=0.043$ vs.\\ real instruction), exceeding the $0.03$ significance threshold.\n222\tCritically, random instruction performs \\emph{worse} than no instruction ($\\Delta=-0.044$), ruling out a simple ``any input helps'' explanation.\n223\t\n224\t\\begin{table}[t]\n225\t\\centering\n226\t\\caption{Instruction condition comparison. Real and no-instruction conditions are indistinguishable, but random-token instructions actively degrade action alignment.}\n227\t\\label{tab:instruction}\n228\t\\small\n229\t\\begin{tabular}{lccr}\n230\t\\toprule\n231\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Peak RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Peak Layer} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs Real} \\\\\n232\t\\midrule\n233\tReal instruction (trained) & 0.488 & 9  & --- \\\\\n234\tReal instruction (untrained) & 0.485 & 12 & --- \\\\\n235\tNo instruction (trained) & 0.489 & 30 & $+$0.001 \\\\\n236\tNo instruction (untrained) & 0.480 & 12 & $-$0.004 \\\\\n237\tRandom instruction (trained) & 0.445 & 12 & $-$0.043 \\\\\n238\tRandom instruction (untrained) & 0.440 & 10 & $-$0.045 \\\\\n239\t\\midrule\n240\tDINOv2 & 0.388 & --- & $-$0.100 \\\\\n241\t\\bottomrule\n242\t\\end{tabular}\n243\t\\end{table}\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[t]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\caption{Instruction effect decomposition. The $\\Delta>0.03$ for random vs.\\ real/no instruction violates strict instruction invariance.}\n248\t\\label{tab:inst_decomp}\n249\t\\small\n250\t\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n251\t\\toprule\n252\t\\textbf{Comparison} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ RSA} & \\textbf{Significant?} \\\\\n253\t\\midrule\n254\tReal vs No instruction & 0.004 & No ($< 0.03$) \\\\\n255\tReal vs Random instruction & 0.043 & Yes ($> 0.03$) \\\\\n256\tNo vs Random instruction & 0.040 & Yes ($> 0.03$) \\\\\n257\tRandom vs DINO & 0.057 & Yes \\\\\n258\t\\bottomrule\n259\t\\end{tabular}\n260\t\\end{table}\n261\t\n262\t\\paragraph{Interpretation.}\n263\tThe LLM does not leverage meaningful instruction content for action structure---consistent with the redundancy framing.\n264\tHowever, random tokens are not neutral: they activate language processing pathways that produce out-of-distribution activations, which compete with vision tokens in attention and dilute action-relevant signals.\n265\tWe therefore weaken Claim~3 from ``instruction invariance'' to: \\emph{meaningful instruction content is not utilized for action structure, but adversarial or random instruction can actively degrade it}.\n266\tThis has practical implications: deployed VLAs should either enforce instruction quality or bypass the LLM entirely to eliminate this vulnerability surface.\n267\t\n268\t\n269\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n270\t\\subsection{Experiment 3: CKA Verification and Metric Dependency}\n271\t\\label{sec:exp3}\n272\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n273\t\n274\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n275\tWe replicate the core component-level analysis using linear CKA as an alternative to RSA, to test whether our conclusions are robust to metric choice.\n276\t\n277\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n278\tTable~\\ref{tab:cka_rsa} reports the comparison.\n279\tBoth metrics agree on the overall ordering (SigLIP+projector $>$ VLA $>$ DINO) and on training invariance ($\\Delta\\leq0.01$) and instruction invariance ($\\Delta\\leq0.02$).\n280\tHowever, they \\emph{disagree} on the magnitude of the LLM's effect: RSA measures a SigLIP-vs-VLA gap of $\\Delta=0.127$, while CKA reduces it to $\\Delta=0.02$---statistically indistinguishable.\n281\t\n282\t\\begin{table}[t]\n283\t\\centering\n284\t\\caption{RSA vs CKA ordering comparison. Ordinal agreement is strong, but the magnitude of the LLM effect is metric-dependent.}\n285\t\\label{tab:cka_rsa}\n286\t\\small\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{RSA Rank} & \\textbf{CKA} & \\textbf{CKA Rank} \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tSigLIP+projector & 0.615 & 1 & 0.52 & 1 \\\\\n292\tVLA trained+inst & 0.488 & 2 & 0.50 & 2 \\\\\n293\tVLA trained no-inst & 0.489 & 2 & 0.50 & 2 \\\\\n294\tVLA untrained+inst & 0.485 & 3 & 0.49 & 3 \\\\\n295\tVLA untrained no-inst & 0.480 & 4 & 0.48 & 4 \\\\\n296\tDINOv2 & 0.388 & 5 & 0.35 & 5 \\\\\n297\t\\bottomrule\n298\t\\end{tabular}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\t\\begin{table}[t]\n302\t\\centering\n303\t\\caption{Metric agreement analysis. The key inconsistency is on the LLM degradation claim, where CKA shows only a marginal gap.}\n304\t\\label{tab:metric_agreement}\n305\t\\small\n306\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n307\t\\toprule\n308\t\\textbf{Claim} & \\textbf{RSA} & \\textbf{CKA} & \\textbf{Agreement} \\\\\n309\t\\midrule\n310\tVision encoder dominance & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.227) & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.17) & Consistent \\\\\n311\tLLM effect (SigLIP$\\to$VLA) & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.127) & Marginal ($\\Delta$=0.02) & Inconsistent \\\\\n312\tTraining invariance & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.003) & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.01) & Consistent \\\\\n313\tInstruction invariance & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.004) & Yes ($\\Delta$=0.02) & Consistent \\\\\n314\t\\bottomrule\n315\t\\end{tabular}\n316\t\\end{table}\n317\t\n318\t\\paragraph{Interpretation.}\n319\tThe discrepancy arises because RSA measures rank correlation of pairwise distances and is sensitive to local geometric distortions, while CKA measures kernel alignment and absorbs affine transformations.\n320\tThe LLM's transformation primarily consists of local geometric restructuring (rotating and scaling representations for next-token prediction), which RSA penalizes but CKA tolerates.\n321\t\n322\tThis has two implications.\n323\tFirst, the strongest defensible claim under both metrics is that \\emph{the LLM at best preserves, and does not enhance, action-relevant structure}---rather than the stronger ``degrades'' formulation.\n324\tSecond, which representational similarity metric is most appropriate for evaluating action alignment is itself an open methodological question.\n325\tWe argue RSA's rank-based nature is better suited to action structure (where ordinal relationships matter), but acknowledge CKA provides a valid alternative perspective.\n326\t\n327\t\n328\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n329\t\\subsection{Experiment 4: Cross-Benchmark Generalization (LIBERO-Spatial)}\n330\t\\label{sec:exp4}\n331\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n334\tWe replicate the full analysis on LIBERO-Spatial, a benchmark emphasizing spatial relationships rather than temporal sequences or goal specifications.\n335\t\n336\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n337\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} reports cross-benchmark results.\n338\tThe ordering SigLIP $\\gg$ VLA $\\approx$ VLA\\textsubscript{untrained} $\\gg$ DINO is preserved across all three benchmarks.\n339\tLIBERO-Spatial shows slightly lower absolute RSA values (SigLIP: $0.58$ vs.\\ $0.615$ on Goal), consistent with the increased complexity of spatial action structure.\n340\tThe U-shape (monotonicity Spearman $=-0.15$) and instruction invariance ($\\Delta=0.005$) also replicate.\n341\t\n342\t\\begin{table}[t]\n343\t\\centering\n344\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark comparison. Core ordering and phenomena replicate across all three LIBERO benchmarks.}\n345\t\\label{tab:cross_benchmark}\n346\t\\small\n347\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n348\t\\toprule\n349\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Goal} & \\textbf{Long} & \\textbf{Spatial} \\\\\n350\t\\midrule\n351\tSigLIP+projector & 0.615 & --- & 0.580 \\\\\n352\tVLA trained & 0.488 & 0.488 & 0.470 \\\\\n353\tVLA untrained & 0.485 & 0.485 & 0.460 \\\\\n354\tDINOv2 & 0.388 & 0.388 & 0.360 \\\\\n355\t\\bottomrule\n356\t\\end{tabular}\n357\t\\end{table}\n358\t\n359\t\\begin{table}[t]\n360\t\\centering\n361\t\\caption{Ordering consistency across benchmarks.}\n362\t\\label{tab:ordering_consistency}\n363\t\\small\n364\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n365\t\\toprule\n366\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Ordering} & \\textbf{U-shape?} & \\textbf{Inst.\\ $\\Delta$} \\\\\n367\t\\midrule\n368\tLIBERO-Goal & SigLIP $\\gg$ VLA $\\approx$ VLA\\textsubscript{u} $\\gg$ DINO & Yes & 0.004 \\\\\n369\tLIBERO-Long & SigLIP $\\gg$ VLA $\\approx$ VLA\\textsubscript{u} $\\gg$ DINO & Yes & 0.004 \\\\\n370\tLIBERO-Spatial & SigLIP $\\gg$ VLA $\\approx$ VLA\\textsubscript{u} $\\gg$ DINO & Yes & 0.005 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\t\\paragraph{Interpretation.}\n376\tThe consistency across three benchmarks with distinct task structures strengthens the generalizability of our findings within the OpenVLA architecture.\n377\tThe slight attenuation of absolute RSA values on LIBERO-Spatial is expected, as spatial manipulation involves more distributed action structure, but does not alter the qualitative conclusions.\n378\t\n379\t\n380\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n381\t\\subsection{Experiment 5: CLIP-ViT Baseline---Isolating the Pretraining Confound}\n382\t\\label{sec:exp5}\n383\t%---------------------------------------------------------------------\n384\t\n385\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n386\tA key confound is that SigLIP and DINOv2 differ in both architecture and pretraining paradigm.\n387\tTo isolate the contribution of vision-language contrastive pretraining, we evaluate CLIP-ViT-L/14 (contrastive pretraining on LAION, same paradigm as SigLIP but different data and architecture).\n388\t\n389\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n390\tTable~\\ref{tab:clip} reports the results.\n391\tCLIP achieves $\\rho=0.56$, substantially above DINOv2 ($0.388$, $\\Delta=+0.172$) but below SigLIP ($0.615$, $\\Delta=-0.055$).\n392\tThis decomposes the SigLIP-DINO gap ($\\Delta=0.227$) into a vision-language pretraining component (76\\%, attributable to CLIP matching most of SigLIP's advantage) and a SigLIP-specific component (24\\%, likely due to training data scale: WebLI vs.\\ LAION).\n393\t\n394\t\\begin{table}[t]\n395\t\\centering\n396\t\\caption{Vision encoder comparison with pretraining control. CLIP-ViT confirms that vision-language pretraining, not SigLIP-specific factors, drives most of the action alignment advantage.}\n397\t\\label{tab:clip}\n398\t\\small\n399\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n400\t\\toprule\n401\t\\textbf{Encoder} & \\textbf{Pretraining} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs DINO} \\\\\n402\t\\midrule\n403\tSigLIP-SO400M & image-text contrastive (WebLI) & 0.615 & $+$0.227 \\\\\n404\tCLIP-ViT-L/14 & image-text contrastive (LAION) & 0.560 & $+$0.172 \\\\\n405\tDINOv2-L & self-supervised (LVD-142M) & 0.388 & --- \\\\\n406\t\\bottomrule\n407\t\\end{tabular}\n408\t\\end{table}\n409\t\n410\t\\begin{table}[t]\n411\t\\centering\n412\t\\caption{Confound decomposition. Vision-language pretraining accounts for the majority of the gap.}\n413\t\\label{tab:confound}\n414\t\\small\n415\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n416\t\\toprule\n417\t\\textbf{Component} & \\textbf{RSA contribution} & \\textbf{Fraction} \\\\\n418\t\\midrule\n419\tVision-language pretraining effect & 0.172 & 76\\% \\\\\n420\tSigLIP-specific advantage & 0.055 & 24\\% \\\\\n421\t\\midrule\n422\tTotal SigLIP--DINO gap & 0.227 & 100\\% \\\\\n423\t\\bottomrule\n424\t\\end{tabular}\n425\t\\end{table}\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Interpretation.}\n428\tThis result reframes the vision encoder dominance finding: it is \\emph{vision-language pretraining}, not any specific encoder architecture, that is the primary source of action-relevant representational structure.\n429\tThe practical implication is sharpened: investing in better vision-language pretraining is more impactful for action quality than task-specific LLM fine-tuning.\n430\tThe residual 24\\% SigLIP advantage may reflect training data scale differences (WebLI $\\gg$ LAION) or architectural choices (sigmoid loss vs.\\ softmax contrastive loss).\n431\t\n432\t\n433\t%=====================================================================\n434\t\\section{Discussion}\n435\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n436\t%=====================================================================\n437\t\n438\t\\paragraph{The Redundant Bottleneck Interpretation.}\n439\tOur per-layer analysis reveals that the LLM does not monotonically degrade action structure; instead, it implements a lossy-then-recovery transformation with an 85\\% recovery ratio.\n440\tThe simplest explanation is that residual connections allow later layers to recover information that middle layers temporarily suppress during next-token-prediction optimization.\n441\tThe net effect ($\\rho: 0.59\\to0.55$) is a modest attenuation rather than destruction, making the LLM's 32-layer transformation a \\emph{redundant bottleneck} for action structure: computationally expensive but representationally nearly neutral.\n442\t\n443\t\\paragraph{Metric Dependency.}\n444\tThe CKA-RSA discrepancy on the LLM effect magnitude ($\\Delta=0.02$ vs.\\ $0.127$) is not a weakness but a finding.\n445\tIt reveals that the LLM's transformation primarily involves local geometric restructuring that preserves global kernel structure (captured by CKA) while disrupting pairwise distance rankings (captured by RSA).\n446\tWhether this local restructuring matters for downstream action decoding depends on whether the action head is sensitive to rank-order or kernel structure---an empirical question we leave open.\n447\t\n448\t\\paragraph{Instruction Vulnerability.}\n449\tThe random-instruction result ($\\Delta=-0.04$ vs.\\ no instruction) identifies a concrete failure mode: out-of-distribution text tokens activate language pathways that interfere with vision-derived action signals.\n450\tThis is practically relevant for deployed VLAs: noisy or adversarial instructions pose a risk that would be entirely eliminated by architectures that bypass the LLM.\n451\t\n452\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language Pretraining as the Key Factor.}\n453\tThe CLIP baseline reveals that 76\\% of SigLIP's action alignment advantage over DINOv2 comes from the shared vision-language pretraining paradigm.\n454\tThis shifts the architectural recommendation from ``use SigLIP'' to ``use any vision-language pretrained encoder''---a more general and actionable insight.\n455\t\n456\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n457\tOur analysis is limited to one VLA architecture family (OpenVLA-class).\n458\tWhile the three-benchmark replication strengthens internal validity, generalization to architecturally different VLAs (e.g., RT-2, $\\pi_0$) requires further study.\n459\tWe probed a single model scale; the redundancy phenomenon may differ at larger scales where the LLM has more capacity to develop action-specific representations.\n460\tThe U-shape mechanism (residual connections vs.\\ learned recovery) is hypothesized but not ablated.\n461\t\n462\t\n463\t%=====================================================================\n464\t\\section{Conclusion}\n465\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n466\t%=====================================================================\n467\t\n468\tWe presented a systematic representational analysis of an OpenVLA-class VLA, revealing that the LLM backbone functions as a redundant bottleneck for action-relevant structure.\n469\tVision-language pretrained encoders establish action alignment before the LLM, and the LLM's 32-layer transformation is a U-shaped detour that approximately recovers its input structure.\n470\tTask-specific fine-tuning and instruction content contribute negligibly, while random instructions actively degrade performance---identifying a concrete vulnerability.\n471\tThe magnitude of the LLM's effect depends on the choice of representational similarity metric, which we highlight as an open methodological question.\n472\t\n473\tThese findings motivate a rethinking of VLA architecture design: rather than routing all information through a large LLM, future architectures should consider action decoding directly from vision-language encoder outputs, potentially retaining the LLM only for tasks that genuinely require language understanding.\n474\t\n475\t\n476\t%=====================================================================\n477\t% References\n478\t%=====================================================================\n479\t\\small\n480\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n481\t\n482\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n483\t\n484\t\\bibitem[Brohan et~al.(2023)]{brohan2023rt2}\n485\tA.~Brohan, N.~Brown, J.~Carbajal, et~al.\n486\t\\newblock {RT-2}: Vision-language-action models transfer web knowledge to robotic control.\n487\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n488\t\n489\t\\bibitem[Fang et~al.(2025)]{fang2025finetuning}\n490\tT.~Fang, et~al.\n491\t\\newblock Fine-tuning erodes pretrained visual reasoning in large vision-language models.\n492\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n493\t\n494\t\\bibitem[Fei et~al.(2025)]{fei2025liberoplus}\n495\tH.~Fei, et~al.\n496\t\\newblock {LIBERO-Plus}: Benchmarking language grounding in vision-language-action models.\n497\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n498\t\n499\t\\bibitem[Grant et~al.(2026)]{grant2026visual}\n500\tR.~Grant, et~al.\n501\t\\newblock The visual pathway dominates action generation in vision-language-action models.\n502\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICRA}, 2026.\n503\t\n504\t\\bibitem[Hou et~al.(2026)]{hou2026langgap}\n505\tY.~Hou, et~al.\n506\t\\newblock {LangGap}: Do vision-language-action models really understand language?\n507\t\\newblock In \\emph{RSS}, 2026.\n508\t\n509\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2024)]{kim2024openvla}\n510\tM.~Kim, et~al.\n511\t\\newblock {OpenVLA}: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n512\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibitem[Kornblith et~al.(2019)]{kornblith2019cka}\n515\tS.~Kornblith, M.~Norouzi, H.~Lee, and G.~Hinton.\n516\t\\newblock Similarity of neural network representations revisited.\n517\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[Kriegeskorte et~al.(2008)]{kriegeskorte2008rsa}\n520\tN.~Kriegeskorte, M.~Mur, and P.~Bandettini.\n521\t\\newblock Representational similarity analysis---connecting the branches of systems neuroscience.\n522\t\\newblock \\emph{Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience}, 2(4), 2008.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[Liu et~al.(2024)]{liu2024libero}\n525\tB.~Liu, et~al.\n526\t\\newblock {LIBERO}: Benchmarking knowledge transfer for lifelong robot learning.\n527\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[Oquab et~al.(2024)]{oquab2024dinov2}\n530\tM.~Oquab, et~al.\n531\t\\newblock {DINOv2}: Learning robust visual features without supervision.\n532\t\\newblock \\emph{TMLR}, 2024.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[Radford et~al.(2021)]{radford2021clip}\n535\tA.~Radford, et~al.\n536\t\\newblock Learning transferable visual models from natural language supervision.\n537\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2021.\n538\t\n539\t\\end{thebibliography}\n540\t\n541\t\\end{document}\n542\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFrS9JYr5QQdkwMT9ueEb4","session_id":"b41b243d-0e5c-47b1-83bb-7de38ac1f796","uuid":"288abd70-97a4-41c2-afc6-b34b41f64fff","timestamp":"2026-05-15T23:07:35.412Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a5c54368334bb6bb1","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFrS9JYr5QQdkwMT9ueEb4","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":72367,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":120698},"uuid":"a61cf497-2f6e-4158-8023-f0fb61f5ec4e","session_id":"b41b243d-0e5c-47b1-83bb-7de38ac1f796"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFrS9JYr5QQdkwMT9ueEb4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me verify the recovery ratio math mentioned in the review prompt and check for other numerical inconsistencies.\n\nThe prompt says: Recovery ratio = (L32-trough)/(L0-trough) = (0.55-0.38)/(0.59-0.38) = 0.17/0.21 = 0.809... but the paper claims 85%.\n\nLet me also check if there are any other discrepancies between the \"best\" paper (which uses monotonic degradation) and the \"worst\" paper (which uses U-shape).\n\nNow I have all the information needed to write the review. The paper I'm reviewing matches the \"worst\" tier dream report -- the one with the U-shape, CLIP-ViT baseline, metric dependency, etc. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"The LLM as a Redundant Bottleneck: Representational Analysis of Action Structure in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n**Reviewer R1 -- Methods and Experiments Focus**\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper performs representational analysis (RSA and CKA) on an OpenVLA-class VLA across three LIBERO benchmarks, decomposing contributions of the vision encoder (SigLIP), projector, and LLM backbone. The central finding is that the frozen SigLIP encoder + projector achieves the highest action alignment (RSA rho=0.615), exceeding the full VLA (rho~0.488), with the LLM exhibiting a U-shaped per-layer RSA trajectory (trough at layer 16, 85% recovery by layer 32). Training and meaningful instruction content have negligible effects on representational structure, though random instructions degrade it. A CLIP-ViT baseline attributes 76% of SigLIP's advantage over DINOv2 to vision-language pretraining.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured experimental design.** The 2x2 factorial (trained/untrained x with/without instruction) cleanly isolates the contributions of fine-tuning and instruction conditioning. The addition of random instruction controls goes beyond a simple binary ablation.\n\n- **Systematic component-level decomposition.** Measuring RSA at projector output, every LLM layer, and the final output provides genuinely novel mechanistic insight into what the LLM does to action-relevant representations. No prior work has performed this layer-by-layer analysis for VLAs.\n\n- **Honest treatment of metric dependency.** Rather than burying the CKA-RSA discrepancy, the paper foregrounds it as a finding. The SigLIP-vs-VLA gap shrinking from Delta=0.127 (RSA) to Delta=0.02 (CKA) is important for the community to understand, and the paper discusses it transparently.\n\n- **CLIP-ViT pretraining control.** The 76% decomposition is a useful contribution that sharpens the practical recommendation from \"use SigLIP\" to \"use any vision-language pretrained encoder.\"\n\n- **Cross-benchmark replication.** Consistent ordering across LIBERO-Goal, Long, and Spatial provides reasonable evidence that findings are not benchmark-specific artifacts.\n\n- **Tables are clear and well-organized.** The 10-table structure makes the quantitative evidence easy to follow.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): No behavioral validation.** This is the most significant gap. RSA and CKA measure representational structure, not task performance. The paper's central architectural implication -- that \"VLA architectures can be substantially simplified\" by decoding actions directly from projector outputs -- is unsupported by any success-rate comparison. Does a projector-only policy actually achieve comparable manipulation success? Without this evidence, the claim that the LLM is \"redundant\" is restricted to the representational level and may not hold at the behavioral level. The LLM could contribute to action generation through mechanisms orthogonal to the geometric structure that RSA/CKA capture (e.g., sequential decision-making, temporal coherence, action token discretization).\n\n**W2 (Major): Single architecture, single scale.** All experiments use one OpenVLA checkpoint with a 32-layer Llama-2-7B backbone. The \"redundant bottleneck\" claim is presented as an architectural property, but it may be specific to this scale. Larger LLMs (13B, 70B) could have sufficient capacity to develop action-specific representations beyond what the vision encoder provides. RT-2, pi-0, and Octo use fundamentally different integration strategies (cross-attention, flow matching, etc.). The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but the title and framing generalize beyond what the evidence supports.\n\n**W3 (Major): Recovery ratio arithmetic error.** The paper states the recovery ratio is 85% (Table 2). By the paper's own definition -- (L32 - trough)/(L0 - trough) -- the computation is (0.55 - 0.38)/(0.59 - 0.38) = 0.17/0.21 = 0.81 (81%), not 85%. This 4-percentage-point discrepancy undermines confidence in the quantitative precision of other reported values. The authors should clarify whether this is a rounding issue from more decimal places or a genuine error.\n\n**W4 (Major): Inadequate statistical testing.** The paper uses a Delta>0.03 threshold to determine \"significance\" of RSA differences but never formally justifies this threshold. Where does 0.03 come from? No permutation tests, no bootstrap confidence intervals (except one mention in the instruction table), and no multiple-comparison corrections are reported. For the core SigLIP-vs-VLA comparison (Delta=0.127 by RSA, Delta=0.02 by CKA), neither formal test nor CI is provided. For the random instruction effect (Delta=0.043), calling it \"significant\" based solely on exceeding an ad-hoc threshold, without a p-value or permutation distribution, is insufficient for a methods-focused paper. Sample sizes for RSA computation (how many observations N, how many pairwise distances) are never specified.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): 76% pretraining decomposition assumes linearity.** The confound decomposition (CLIP-DINO)/(SigLIP-DINO) = 0.172/0.227 = 76% assumes RSA differences are linearly decomposable. But RSA rho is a Spearman rank correlation, which is nonlinear. The \"76%\" figure is an intuitive ratio, not a principled variance decomposition. Presenting it as a definitive attribution of \"vision-language pretraining effect\" overstates the inferential strength.\n\n**W6 (Moderate): U-shape mechanism is hypothesized but not ablated.** The paper attributes the U-shape recovery to residual connections and layer normalization (Section 4.1, Discussion), but this is speculation. Ablating residual connections for a subset of layers (e.g., zeroing skip connections in layers 16-32) would directly test this hypothesis. Without ablation, the U-shape is a descriptive observation, not a mechanistic explanation.\n\n**W7 (Minor-to-Moderate): No figures.** The paper contains zero figures -- only tables. A per-layer RSA line plot (the \"U-shape\") would be far more visually compelling and interpretable than Table 1. Similarly, bar charts for the instruction comparison, CKA-RSA scatter plots, and cross-benchmark panels are conspicuously absent. For a CoRL paper, visual communication is essential for robotics practitioners.\n\n**W8 (Minor): LIBERO-Long missing SigLIP+projector entry.** Table 7 (cross-benchmark comparison) shows \"---\" for SigLIP+projector on LIBERO-Long. This is the paper's primary benchmark, and the missing entry weakens the cross-benchmark comparison.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** What is the sample size N used for RSA computation? How many observations contribute to each pairwise distance matrix, and how were they sampled across tasks and trajectories? RSA results can be highly sensitive to N.\n\n**Q2.** The recovery ratio appears to be 81% by your formula, not 85%. Can you clarify the exact computation, including any intermediate decimal precision?\n\n**Q3.** Have you attempted to train a policy head directly on projector outputs (bypassing the LLM) and measured task success rates? If the LLM is truly a \"redundant bottleneck,\" a projector-only policy should achieve comparable success rates. Without this, the practical implications in Section 5 are speculative.\n\n**Q4.** The Delta>0.03 significance threshold: what is its provenance? Is it derived from a permutation null distribution, a bootstrap analysis, or chosen ad hoc? If the latter, how sensitive are your significance conclusions (especially for random instructions, Delta=0.043) to this choice?\n\n**Q5.** The CKA analysis shows the SigLIP-VLA gap is only Delta=0.02, which you describe as \"statistically indistinguishable.\" Under CKA, the LLM is not even a redundant bottleneck -- it is essentially a structure-preserving transformation. How do you reconcile the \"redundant bottleneck\" framing (which emphasizes the U-shape and net degradation) with a metric under which there is no meaningful degradation at all?\n\n**Q6.** The Spearman monotonicity of -0.18 (Table 2) is very far from the -0.88 reported in the best-case analysis (also from your group's experiments, based on project records). Can you explain this discrepancy? Were different aggregation methods, feature keys, or subsets used?\n\n**Q7.** For the CLIP-ViT baseline, were CLIP features extracted from the same observations using the same preprocessing pipeline as SigLIP? Differences in input resolution, normalization, or tokenization could confound the comparison.\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper references \"Figure 1\" (perlayer) in passing but no figure is included. If figures are intended for a camera-ready version, placeholder references should be removed.\n- Table 7 uses \"---\" without explanation for the LIBERO-Long SigLIP entry. A footnote explaining why this is missing would help.\n- The claim that \"all findings replicate across LIBERO-Goal, Long, Spatial\" is overstated when LIBERO-Long has missing entries and the U-shape/instruction analyses are not shown separately for each benchmark.\n- The reference list includes several incomplete entries (e.g., \"et al.\" without full author lists, missing page numbers for conference papers). CoRL requires complete bibliographic information.\n- The paper uses \"33-layer\" and \"32-layer\" interchangeably to describe the LLM. The text should be consistent (OpenVLA uses Llama-2-7B which has 32 transformer layers; the projector output is sometimes counted as \"layer 0,\" yielding 33 probing points).\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 |\n| **Presentation** | 2/4 |\n| **Contribution** | 3/4 |\n| **Overall** | 4/10 |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 |\n\n**Justification:**\n\n*Soundness (2/4):* The core representational analysis is reasonable and the experimental design is thoughtful, but the absence of formal statistical testing, the arithmetic error in the recovery ratio, the ad-hoc significance threshold, and the linear decomposition assumption weaken confidence in the quantitative claims. The metric dependency (RSA vs CKA disagreeing on the central claim) is acknowledged but not resolved.\n\n*Presentation (2/4):* Tables are clear, but the complete absence of figures is a serious deficiency for a paper whose headline finding is a \"U-shape\" -- a fundamentally visual concept. The paper would benefit enormously from 3-4 well-designed figures.\n\n*Contribution (3/4):* The question \"where do action-relevant representations come from in VLAs?\" is timely and important. The component-level decomposition and the metric-dependency finding are genuine contributions. The CLIP pretraining control adds value. However, without behavioral validation, the practical implications remain speculative.\n\n*Overall (4/10):* The paper asks the right question and collects interesting data, but the execution has significant methodological gaps (no behavioral validation, no formal statistics, arithmetic errors, metric-dependent conclusions). In its current form, the conclusions are not sufficiently supported for a top venue. With behavioral experiments (projector-only policy success rates), proper statistical testing, figures, and resolution of the quantitative errors, this could become a solid contribution.\n\n*Confidence (4/5):* I have carefully reviewed the experimental design, checked the reported numbers against the stated formulas, and am familiar with RSA/CKA methodology and the VLA literature. My main uncertainty is whether I am missing additional supplementary materials not included in the submission."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5c54368334bb6bb1 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5c54368334bb6bb1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 75127\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 120701</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b41b243d-0e5c-47b1-83bb-7de38ac1f796","uuid":"b7cc9140-f382-4a99-877f-3bac685d2038","timestamp":"2026-05-15T23:08:49.297Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Write an independent, detailed review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"The LLM as a Redundant Bottleneck: Representational Analysis of Action Structure in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThis paper performs RSA and CKA representational analysis on an OpenVLA-class VLA (SigLIP vision encoder + projector + 32-layer LLM) across three LIBERO benchmarks. Key findings:\n1. SigLIP+projector achieves highest action alignment (RSA ρ=0.615), exceeding full VLA (ρ≈0.488). CLIP-ViT baseline (ρ=0.56) attributes 76% of SigLIP-DINO gap to vision-language pretraining.\n2. Per-layer RSA shows U-shape: drops from 0.59 (projector) to 0.38 (layer 16), recovers to 0.55 (layer 32). Spearman monotonicity = -0.18, recovery ratio = 85%.\n3. Real vs no instruction: Δ=0.004 (not significant). Random instruction: Δ=0.043 (significant degradation).\n4. CKA reduces SigLIP-vs-VLA gap from Δ=0.127 (RSA) to Δ=0.02, making degradation claim metric-dependent.\n5. All findings replicate across LIBERO-Goal, Long, Spatial.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included 5 experiments:\n- Exp 1: Per-layer RSA curve (plan_003)\n- Exp 2: Random instruction control (plan_008)  \n- Exp 3: CKA verification (plan_009)\n- Exp 4: LIBERO-Spatial cross-benchmark (plan_010)\n- Exp 5: CLIP-ViT baseline (not in original plan, added as blocking)\n\nAll 5 experiments appear in the paper.\n\n## Review Dimensions (CoRL)\nScore each on 1-10 OpenReview scale. Focus your review on:\n\n### Methodological Rigor\n- Is RSA (Spearman rank correlation on pairwise distance matrices) + CKA (linear kernel alignment) an appropriate and sufficient toolkit?\n- The \"significance threshold\" of Δ>0.03 for RSA differences: is this justified? Are there formal statistical tests (permutation tests, bootstrap CIs)?\n- Sample sizes for RSA computation are not specified in the paper.\n- Recovery ratio definition: (L32-trough)/(L0-trough) = (0.55-0.38)/(0.59-0.38) = 0.81, but paper says 85%. Check this math.\n- The 76% pretraining confound decomposition assumes linearity (CLIP-DINO)/(SigLIP-DINO). Is this valid?\n\n### Experimental Completeness\n- Only one VLA architecture (OpenVLA-class). No RT-2, π0, Octo, etc.\n- Single model scale (32-layer LLM). Would a larger LLM show the same redundancy?\n- U-shape mechanism hypothesized as residual connections but NOT ablated.\n- CRITICAL: No behavioral validation. RSA/CKA measure representational alignment, not task performance. Where is the evidence that a projector-only policy actually achieves comparable success rates?\n- LIBERO-Long is missing SigLIP+projector entry in Table 7 (\"—\").\n- All experiments are in simulation (LIBERO), no real-robot experiments.\n\n### Presentation of Results\n- Tables are clear and well-organized (10 tables total).\n- No figures in the paper — only tables. Missing the U-shape line plot, instruction comparison bar chart, CKA scatter plot, cross-benchmark panels, and CLIP decomposition bar chart that would make the findings more visually compelling.\n- Statistical error bars / confidence intervals not reported for any RSA or CKA values.\n\nWrite your review in this format:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1-W6+)\n4. Questions for Authors (numbered Q1-Q5+)\n5. Minor Issues\n6. Scores: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-10), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is for CoRL (robotics + learning venue, ~28% acceptance rate).","agentId":"a5c54368334bb6bb1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me verify the recovery ratio math mentioned in the review prompt and check for other numerical inconsistencies.\n\nThe prompt says: Recovery ratio = (L32-trough)/(L0-trough) = (0.55-0.38)/(0.59-0.38) = 0.17/0.21 = 0.809... but the paper claims 85%.\n\nLet me also check if there are any other discrepancies between the \"best\" paper (which uses monotonic degradation) and the \"worst\" paper (which uses U-shape).\n\nNow I have all the information needed to write the review. The paper I'm reviewing matches the \"worst\" tier dream report -- the one with the U-shape, CLIP-ViT baseline, metric dependency, etc. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"The LLM as a Redundant Bottleneck: Representational Analysis of Action Structure in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n**Reviewer R1 -- Methods and Experiments Focus**\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper performs representational analysis (RSA and CKA) on an OpenVLA-class VLA across three LIBERO benchmarks, decomposing contributions of the vision encoder (SigLIP), projector, and LLM backbone. The central finding is that the frozen SigLIP encoder + projector achieves the highest action alignment (RSA rho=0.615), exceeding the full VLA (rho~0.488), with the LLM exhibiting a U-shaped per-layer RSA trajectory (trough at layer 16, 85% recovery by layer 32). Training and meaningful instruction content have negligible effects on representational structure, though random instructions degrade it. A CLIP-ViT baseline attributes 76% of SigLIP's advantage over DINOv2 to vision-language pretraining.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured experimental design.** The 2x2 factorial (trained/untrained x with/without instruction) cleanly isolates the contributions of fine-tuning and instruction conditioning. The addition of random instruction controls goes beyond a simple binary ablation.\n\n- **Systematic component-level decomposition.** Measuring RSA at projector output, every LLM layer, and the final output provides genuinely novel mechanistic insight into what the LLM does to action-relevant representations. No prior work has performed this layer-by-layer analysis for VLAs.\n\n- **Honest treatment of metric dependency.** Rather than burying the CKA-RSA discrepancy, the paper foregrounds it as a finding. The SigLIP-vs-VLA gap shrinking from Delta=0.127 (RSA) to Delta=0.02 (CKA) is important for the community to understand, and the paper discusses it transparently.\n\n- **CLIP-ViT pretraining control.** The 76% decomposition is a useful contribution that sharpens the practical recommendation from \"use SigLIP\" to \"use any vision-language pretrained encoder.\"\n\n- **Cross-benchmark replication.** Consistent ordering across LIBERO-Goal, Long, and Spatial provides reasonable evidence that findings are not benchmark-specific artifacts.\n\n- **Tables are clear and well-organized.** The 10-table structure makes the quantitative evidence easy to follow.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): No behavioral validation.** This is the most significant gap. RSA and CKA measure representational structure, not task performance. The paper's central architectural implication -- that \"VLA architectures can be substantially simplified\" by decoding actions directly from projector outputs -- is unsupported by any success-rate comparison. Does a projector-only policy actually achieve comparable manipulation success? Without this evidence, the claim that the LLM is \"redundant\" is restricted to the representational level and may not hold at the behavioral level. The LLM could contribute to action generation through mechanisms orthogonal to the geometric structure that RSA/CKA capture (e.g., sequential decision-making, temporal coherence, action token discretization).\n\n**W2 (Major): Single architecture, single scale.** All experiments use one OpenVLA checkpoint with a 32-layer Llama-2-7B backbone. The \"redundant bottleneck\" claim is presented as an architectural property, but it may be specific to this scale. Larger LLMs (13B, 70B) could have sufficient capacity to develop action-specific representations beyond what the vision encoder provides. RT-2, pi-0, and Octo use fundamentally different integration strategies (cross-attention, flow matching, etc.). The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but the title and framing generalize beyond what the evidence supports.\n\n**W3 (Major): Recovery ratio arithmetic error.** The paper states the recovery ratio is 85% (Table 2). By the paper's own definition -- (L32 - trough)/(L0 - trough) -- the computation is (0.55 - 0.38)/(0.59 - 0.38) = 0.17/0.21 = 0.81 (81%), not 85%. This 4-percentage-point discrepancy undermines confidence in the quantitative precision of other reported values. The authors should clarify whether this is a rounding issue from more decimal places or a genuine error.\n\n**W4 (Major): Inadequate statistical testing.** The paper uses a Delta>0.03 threshold to determine \"significance\" of RSA differences but never formally justifies this threshold. Where does 0.03 come from? No permutation tests, no bootstrap confidence intervals (except one mention in the instruction table), and no multiple-comparison corrections are reported. For the core SigLIP-vs-VLA comparison (Delta=0.127 by RSA, Delta=0.02 by CKA), neither formal test nor CI is provided. For the random instruction effect (Delta=0.043), calling it \"significant\" based solely on exceeding an ad-hoc threshold, without a p-value or permutation distribution, is insufficient for a methods-focused paper. Sample sizes for RSA computation (how many observations N, how many pairwise distances) are never specified.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): 76% pretraining decomposition assumes linearity.** The confound decomposition (CLIP-DINO)/(SigLIP-DINO) = 0.172/0.227 = 76% assumes RSA differences are linearly decomposable. But RSA rho is a Spearman rank correlation, which is nonlinear. The \"76%\" figure is an intuitive ratio, not a principled variance decomposition. Presenting it as a definitive attribution of \"vision-language pretraining effect\" overstates the inferential strength.\n\n**W6 (Moderate): U-shape mechanism is hypothesized but not ablated.** The paper attributes the U-shape recovery to residual connections and layer normalization (Section 4.1, Discussion), but this is speculation. Ablating residual connections for a subset of layers (e.g., zeroing skip connections in layers 16-32) would directly test this hypothesis. Without ablation, the U-shape is a descriptive observation, not a mechanistic explanation.\n\n**W7 (Minor-to-Moderate): No figures.** The paper contains zero figures -- only tables. A per-layer RSA line plot (the \"U-shape\") would be far more visually compelling and interpretable than Table 1. Similarly, bar charts for the instruction comparison, CKA-RSA scatter plots, and cross-benchmark panels are conspicuously absent. For a CoRL paper, visual communication is essential for robotics practitioners.\n\n**W8 (Minor): LIBERO-Long missing SigLIP+projector entry.** Table 7 (cross-benchmark comparison) shows \"---\" for SigLIP+projector on LIBERO-Long. This is the paper's primary benchmark, and the missing entry weakens the cross-benchmark comparison.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** What is the sample size N used for RSA computation? How many observations contribute to each pairwise distance matrix, and how were they sampled across tasks and trajectories? RSA results can be highly sensitive to N.\n\n**Q2.** The recovery ratio appears to be 81% by your formula, not 85%. Can you clarify the exact computation, including any intermediate decimal precision?\n\n**Q3.** Have you attempted to train a policy head directly on projector outputs (bypassing the LLM) and measured task success rates? If the LLM is truly a \"redundant bottleneck,\" a projector-only policy should achieve comparable success rates. Without this, the practical implications in Section 5 are speculative.\n\n**Q4.** The Delta>0.03 significance threshold: what is its provenance? Is it derived from a permutation null distribution, a bootstrap analysis, or chosen ad hoc? If the latter, how sensitive are your significance conclusions (especially for random instructions, Delta=0.043) to this choice?\n\n**Q5.** The CKA analysis shows the SigLIP-VLA gap is only Delta=0.02, which you describe as \"statistically indistinguishable.\" Under CKA, the LLM is not even a redundant bottleneck -- it is essentially a structure-preserving transformation. How do you reconcile the \"redundant bottleneck\" framing (which emphasizes the U-shape and net degradation) with a metric under which there is no meaningful degradation at all?\n\n**Q6.** The Spearman monotonicity of -0.18 (Table 2) is very far from the -0.88 reported in the best-case analysis (also from your group's experiments, based on project records). Can you explain this discrepancy? Were different aggregation methods, feature keys, or subsets used?\n\n**Q7.** For the CLIP-ViT baseline, were CLIP features extracted from the same observations using the same preprocessing pipeline as SigLIP? Differences in input resolution, normalization, or tokenization could confound the comparison.\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper references \"Figure 1\" (perlayer) in passing but no figure is included. If figures are intended for a camera-ready version, placeholder references should be removed.\n- Table 7 uses \"---\" without explanation for the LIBERO-Long SigLIP entry. A footnote explaining why this is missing would help.\n- The claim that \"all findings replicate across LIBERO-Goal, Long, Spatial\" is overstated when LIBERO-Long has missing entries and the U-shape/instruction analyses are not shown separately for each benchmark.\n- The reference list includes several incomplete entries (e.g., \"et al.\" without full author lists, missing page numbers for conference papers). CoRL requires complete bibliographic information.\n- The paper uses \"33-layer\" and \"32-layer\" interchangeably to describe the LLM. The text should be consistent (OpenVLA uses Llama-2-7B which has 32 transformer layers; the projector output is sometimes counted as \"layer 0,\" yielding 33 probing points).\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 |\n| **Presentation** | 2/4 |\n| **Contribution** | 3/4 |\n| **Overall** | 4/10 |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 |\n\n**Justification:**\n\n*Soundness (2/4):* The core representational analysis is reasonable and the experimental design is thoughtful, but the absence of formal statistical testing, the arithmetic error in the recovery ratio, the ad-hoc significance threshold, and the linear decomposition assumption weaken confidence in the quantitative claims. The metric dependency (RSA vs CKA disagreeing on the central claim) is acknowledged but not resolved.\n\n*Presentation (2/4):* Tables are clear, but the complete absence of figures is a serious deficiency for a paper whose headline finding is a \"U-shape\" -- a fundamentally visual concept. The paper would benefit enormously from 3-4 well-designed figures.\n\n*Contribution (3/4):* The question \"where do action-relevant representations come from in VLAs?\" is timely and important. The component-level decomposition and the metric-dependency finding are genuine contributions. The CLIP pretraining control adds value. However, without behavioral validation, the practical implications remain speculative.\n\n*Overall (4/10):* The paper asks the right question and collects interesting data, but the execution has significant methodological gaps (no behavioral validation, no formal statistics, arithmetic errors, metric-dependent conclusions). In its current form, the conclusions are not sufficiently supported for a top venue. With behavioral experiments (projector-only policy success rates), proper statistical testing, figures, and resolution of the quantitative errors, this could become a solid contribution.\n\n*Confidence (4/5):* I have carefully reviewed the experimental design, checked the reported numbers against the stated formulas, and am familiar with RSA/CKA methodology and the VLA literature. My main uncertainty is whether I am missing additional supplementary materials not included in the submission."}],"totalDurationMs":120701,"totalTokens":75127,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10307,"cache_read_input_tokens":61773,"output_tokens":3046,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10307},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3046,"cache_read_input_tokens":61773,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10307,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":1,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
